diff --git a/doc/_docstrings/objects.Perc.rst b/doc/_docstrings/objects.Perc.rst
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1d32b1b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/_docstrings/objects.Perc.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+.. code:: python
+
+    import seaborn.objects as so
+    import seaborn as sns
+    penguins = sns.load_dataset("penguins")
+
+With the default ``k=5``, the transform returns the minimum, quartiles, and
+maximum of each group:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+    so.Plot(penguins, x="species", y="body_mass_g").add(so.Dots(), so.Perc())
+
+Set an integer ``k`` to request that many evenly spaced levels between 0 and
+100, inclusive:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+    so.Plot(penguins, x="species", y="body_mass_g").add(so.Dots(), so.Perc(k=3))
+
+Or pass the levels directly:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+    so.Plot(penguins, x="species", y="body_mass_g").add(
+        so.Dots(), so.Perc(k=[10, 50, 90])
+    )
+
+The output has one row per requested percentile; it does not represent a point
+estimate or an interval. To draw an interval between the endpoints, combine
+``Perc`` with a range mark:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+    so.Plot(penguins, x="species", y="body_mass_g").add(
+        so.Range(), so.Perc(k=2)
+    )
